{{Elections in Alabama sidebar}}
The '''1996 United States Senate election in Alabama''' was held on November 5, 1996. Incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] U.S. Senator [[Howell Heflin]] decided to retire. [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Jeff Sessions]] won the open seat, becoming the first of his party to win this seat since [[Reconstruction era of the United States|Reconstruction]] in 1868 and only the second Republican ever to be popularly elected to the U.S. Senate from [[Alabama]].

The swearing-in or the inauguration of [[Jeff Sessions]] marked the first time since 1871 that Republicans simultaneously held both Senate seats. This was the first time ever that a Republican won a full term to this Senate seat.

==Background==

In [[1968 United States presidential election|the 1968 presidential election]], Alabama supported native son and [[American Independent Party]] candidate [[George Wallace]] over both [[Richard Nixon]] and [[Hubert Humphrey]]. Wallace was the official [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] candidate in Alabama, while Humphrey was listed as the "National Democratic".<ref>{{cite web | url=http://uselectionatlas.org/RESULTS/state.php?fips=1&year=1968&f=0&off=0&elect=0 | title=1968 Presidential General Election Results - Alabama | publisher=Uselectionatlas.org | date=November 5, 1968 | access-date=August 7, 2009}}</ref> In [[1976 United States presidential election in Alabama|1976]], Democratic candidate [[Jimmy Carter]] from Georgia carried the state, the region, and the nation, but Democratic control of the region slipped after that.

Since 1980, conservative Alabama voters have increasingly voted for Republican candidates at the Federal level, especially in Presidential elections. By contrast, Democratic candidates have been elected to many state-level offices and, until 2010, comprised a longstanding majority in the [[Alabama Legislature]].

Three-term [[incumbent]] [[Howell Heflin]] decided not to seek re-election. A 75-year-old moderate-to-conservative [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]], Heflin was re-elected in 1990 with over 60% of the vote. Until [[2017 United States Senate special election in Alabama|2017]], Richard Shelby’s 1992 victory was the last time Democrats won a U.S. Senate seat in Alabama; Shelby later became a Republican in 1994.

